{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Current treatment strategies for PSD encompass a variety of pharmacological interventions, including antidepressants, anti-inflammatory drugs, and neuroprotective agents [14]. Additionally, psychosocial therapies, such as cognitive behavioral therapy and interpersonal therapy, are utilized to provide comprehensive care [15,16]. However, research has demonstrated that antidepressants exhibit moderate effectiveness for mild to moderate depression and may include side effects that impact the central nervous and gastrointestinal systems [17]. Similarly, the therapeutic benefits of psychotherapy tend to be modest [18]. In this context, exercise interventions have garnered growing interest as a potential alternative treatment. Previous studies suggest that exercise may alleviate depressive symptoms by fostering motor-function recovery, enhancing cortical brain activity, and improving neural responsiveness [19-21]. Existing research indicates that exercise interventions are efficacious in treating depression in non-stroke patients... In stroke populations, various studies have explored the effects of different exercise protocols... However, contrasting results have also been reported... Although a meta-analysis published in 2014 investigated the effects of exercise interventions on PSD [28], it suffered from a small sample size and potential selection bias. Furthermore, a meta-analysis examining home-based training for PSD included joint flexibility training... Moreover, another meta-analysis exploring the impact of multicomponent training on PSD included only four studies... We also observed that, despite most studies investigating the impact of exercise on PSD [28-31], none provided comprehensive details regarding the specific exercise protocols or intervention strategies employed.",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state of knowledge regarding stroke and PSD treatments, highlights uncertainties in existing literature (contrasting results, limitations of previous meta-analyses), and identifies gaps in knowledge regarding exercise protocols."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Stroke patients with depressive symptoms face a threefold higher risk of death over the subsequent ten years compared with those without depressive symptoms [13]. Current treatment strategies for PSD encompass a variety of pharmacological interventions... However, research has demonstrated that antidepressants exhibit moderate effectiveness... Similarly, the therapeutic benefits of psychotherapy tend to be modest... In this context, exercise interventions have garnered growing interest as a potential alternative treatment... Therefore, the objective of this study is to examine the effect of exercise on depressive symptoms in stroke patients, with the aim of identifying the optimal exercise regimen for ameliorating depression in stroke patients.",
      "reason": "The introduction explains the importance of addressing PSD due to its high mortality risk and limitations of current treatments, and explicitly states the study's objective to identify optimal exercise regimens, justifying the need for the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Although a meta-analysis published in 2014 investigated the effects of exercise interventions on PSD [28], it suffered from a small sample size and potential selection bias. Furthermore, a meta-analysis examining home-based training for PSD included joint flexibility training, which had a limited impact on physical functioning, thereby suggesting some reservations about justifying its inclusion in the exercise intervention group [29]. Moreover, another meta-analysis exploring the impact of multicomponent training on PSD included only four studies, resulting in a constrained sample size and reduced reliability [30]. We also observed that, despite most studies investigating the impact of exercise on PSD [28-31], none provided comprehensive details regarding the specific exercise protocols or intervention strategies employed.",
      "reason": "The introduction identifies and cites previous systematic reviews/meta-analyses and explains the need for the current review by detailing their limitations (sample size, bias, inclusion of non-exercise components, lack of protocol details)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_2",
      "quote": "Previous studies suggest that exercise may alleviate depressive symptoms by fostering motor-function recovery, enhancing cortical brain activity, and improving neural responsiveness [19-21].",
      "reason": "The introduction briefly describes the proposed mechanism of action for exercise interventions, citing motor-function recovery, cortical brain activity, and neural responsiveness."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The provided text does not mention or present a logic model to show hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 95
}

